A||||11||drednot||strange suspension sounds / squeeks||||1||
Z||000000||drednot||04-06-2001||07:37 PM||||I have got a wicked rattle in the front and rear suspension of my 1985 Fiero.  When I hit un-even pavement, or small potholes, it makes a loud clunking/rattling sound.  Also, when moving fast over a jump, just as the car leaves the pavement, it makes a loud squeaking sound.  It only happens when the suspension goes from normal riding height(about to go over a jump), to fully extended (flying through the air).  Crashing down and bouncing off the pavement, it has on problem with (except for always bending anti-roll bars).  The other problem is when the car gets up to about 100 mph.  It starts to wonder.  The steering wheel can be completely straight, but the car will kind of drift.  It happens a lot on wavy pavement.  Other than that, it is a great car.  I was thinking it may be the tie rod ends or maybe another bent anti-roll bar.  Anything else it could be?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||04-06-2001||08:34 PM||||Tie rods, tie rod ends, Ball Joints, bad control arm bushing, (dry-rot or corroded control arm), or wheel bearings. All can cause problems.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||sqoach||04-06-2001||08:46 PM||||Uh...is this a joke??? Flying through the air, going 100 mph...what the hell!?<P>Driving like you're the Dukes of Hazzard may be part of the problem.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>           <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach2.JPG">  <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily overheating)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Stoic||04-06-2001||08:55 PM||||What Oreif said is right, but I would put my money on it being the rear tie rod ends.  I had mine done recently, after it was acting in a similar fashion.  Good luck Bo. <P>------------------<BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.||||reg||6
Z||000004||ThatRickGuy||04-06-2001||10:04 PM||||Lower ball joints and control arm bushings up front, they take most of the abuse/rot. not sure about the back end.||||reg||1
Z||000005||drednot||04-07-2001||09:44 AM||||no, this is not a joke.  I don't have the tolls to do the tie rods my self.  Plus the fact that I'm not sure that that is the only thing wrong with the suspension.  I am thinking that I will take it to a reputable shop and have them inspect the suspension, and fix what's wrong.  I'll let you know what they say.  It just can't take the jumps like my dads NSX can.  I wish he would buy me a car like that.  Oh well... He thinks that I should have to work for my money.  the worst thing that happened to my Fiero was when I got a good 12 feet of air on a jump.  it was so fun when it happened, but the landing... lets just say new control arms and tires were in order.||||reg||8
Z||000006||Will||04-07-2001||11:13 AM||||Jack the front of the car up.<BR>Grab the tire with your hands at the 3 & 9 o'clock positions.<BR>Pull with one hand, push with the other, then push with one hand and pull with the other. You shold have the key out of the car (column lock engaged) for this.<BR>If you feel any play, it's too much.<BR>Have someone lie under the car and watch the suspension as you move the tire to see where the looseness is coming from. Replace the part that has play. Find the next part that has play.<BR>Do the same with your hands at the 12 and 6 o'clock positions.<BR>Repeat for rear end.<P>Maybe if you didn't destroy control arms and tires all the time you would have the money to buy the tools to work on your car yourself.||||reg||1
Z||000007||drednot||04-07-2001||05:34 PM||||I did it, yes, there is play.  About a full 1/2 inch to be exact!  The front wheels move 1/2 inch in either direction with the steering centered.  I guess that's what that wondering and clunking is.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||04-07-2001||05:43 PM||||Anybody buying this 12ft of air b/s? NSX doing the same? Are we rally-crossing here?<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 04-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Mach10||04-07-2001||06:16 PM||||*flame* *flame* *flame*<BR>I'm going to agree with sqoach. I think the noise problem is from treating the car like crap. <BR>12ft in the air? Pull the other one. It has bells on.<BR>Daddy's NSX? You are either a liar or a spoilt rich-boy. Either way, you suck.<BR>*flame* *flame* *flame*<P>Now that that's done, I agree, tie-rods, ball joints, or else bad shocks. The wandering will be because that front end will start to rise up from the lift generated by poor aerodynamics. If your tie-rods or balljoints are worn, the wheels will be shimmying around, and at higher speeds, will start to wander. Time for a suspension job.||||reg||8
Z||000010||Will||04-07-2001||07:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by drednot:<BR><B>I did it, yes, there is play.  About a full 1/2 inch to be exact!  The front wheels move 1/2 inch in either direction with the steering centered.  I guess that's what that wondering and clunking is.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Then have someone get under the car and see exactly where it is coming from. If the spindle is moving relative to the upper control arm, then the upper ball joint needs to be replaced, etc. Find all the sources of play and replace worn out parts. You will have to repeat several times, replacing a few parts each time, as no one thing is usually to blame. Replace parts intelligently; don't just throw parts at the car and hope you fix the problem.<P>And remember that it's always going to break if you always abuse it.||||reg||1
Z||000011||GarethWright||04-08-2001||01:23 AM||||Tools aren't a problem if you have an Autozone close to you.  I replaced my front ball joints (upper and lower) and tie rods, all it cost me was parts and the weekend  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Autozone will rent out tools, there's a rather heavy deposit, but you get ALL of it back when you return the tools.<BR>I would recommend having the Suspension looked at by a professional, get a free inspection and quote from somewhere, then get a parts list off of the guy and go to the store.  Take the car back to the suspension guy when you are done, have him do a FULL alignment AND Toe-In adjustment.  If you don't do the Toe-In you will have bump-steer from Hell.<BR>Have fun, take it slow, and expect yur Dads NSX to enjoy fresh air and sunshine on the driveway for at least an afternoon  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
